// Attempt 12: Rebuild from the best (attempt 2, 40.8%) with two targeted span-rescue
// additions, avoiding the three changes that caused attempt 11's catastrophe.
//
// Why attempt 11 regressed catastrophically (6.1% → from 40.8%):
//   1. Penalty 16.0 (vs 4.0): High penalty knocked `!` out of the top slot, causing
//      the model to switch to other garbled tokens. At 4.0, the first `!` gets a
//      modest discount but the model still generates coherent arithmetic on most paths.
//   2. 30-token guard + `steps := maxSteps; break`: This STOPPED all generation after
//      force-closing one intermediate span, preventing the model from continuing to the
//      final answer span. Task rollouts show multiple `<< >>` spans before the answer.
//   3. EOS rescue: Extra complexity that interfered with normal operation.
//
// This attempt adds two loop-rescue guards that CONTINUE (no break, no steps:=maxSteps)
// after force-closing a troubled span, so subsequent spans can still be generated:
//
//   Guard A — 3-consecutive repetition: If the last 3 constrained tokens are identical
//     (e.g., `!`, `!`, `!` or `//`, `//`, `//`), call CloseSpanWithinBudget(30) and
//     continue the outer loop. This rescues `!!!!` loops without killing later spans.
//
//   Guard B — 45-token span length: Catches periodic loops like `//n*days*n` that
//     don't trigger guard A. Call CloseSpanWithinBudget(20) and continue.
//
//   Both guards use `min(X, remaining)` formulations so `steps + budget <= maxSteps`
//   holds cleanly without needing extra assert hints.
//
// Other changes from attempt 2:
//   - closeReserve 80 → 100 (slightly safer budget management)
//   - Prompt guidance: removed `**` (not required by task), added prohibition on
//     `=` and `if`/`else` inside `<< >>` to discourage assignment/conditional patterns.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// Invariant 1 — parser_validity:
//   insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
//
